      "role": "user",
      "content": "It's a bit better, but honestly, it still feels off. Even with the new wording, the European section still feels like the \"main event\" of human progress—all that talk of \"dramatically altering the trajectory of human progress\"—whereas the indigenous part just feels like a preamble or a precursor to what actually mattered.\n\nI'm trying to avoid the subtle implication that indigenous knowledge was just a localized version of \"resource management\" while the European era was the one that truly \"awakened\" humanity. It's like you're still treating them as two different categories of existence rather than two parts of the same continuous human story. \n\nCan you try to bridge them? I want a transition that shows how indigenous scientific methods actually paved the way for or were conceptually similar to the rigorous inquiry we saw later. I want to strip away that sense that one is \"traditional/resourceful\" and the other is \"modern/revolutionary.\" Make the two eras feel like they are driven by the same kind of human drive for mastery over the world.",
